WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.260
This is a virtual reality

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:09.970
exposure therapy session
for treating PTSD.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.670
It's an immersive experience
designed to help patients

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.083
confront trauma from conflict zones.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.640
Virtual reality is, in my field,

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.000
a psychologist's dream to be able to build

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.800
simulated environments, put people in,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.680
observe how they respond,

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.630
help them to respond differently

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.020
to support their therapeutic goals.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.350
That's the essence of why we do therapy

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:36.750
or human research.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:37.780
And this field,

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.810
known as clinical virtual
reality, is growing.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.380
Challenging popular preconceptions of VR

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:46.530
as a novel gaming device
to include advanced medical

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.670
and psychological treatments.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.470
It's being used for conditions ranging

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.740
from dementia to chronic pain,

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.120
as well as providing training

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.480
and remote health care sessions.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:57.313
You know what's amazing about VR,

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:01.500
unlike any other audio
visual medium ever created,

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.600
is how emotionally provocative it is.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.250
Because our brain was never designed

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.520
to live in two realities at once.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.550
Like that's not
evolutionarily required of us.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:15.830
We just accept the
reality that we're given.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.267
And so in VR,

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:22.210
you feel your brain believes
that it is physically present

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.770
in some other world.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:27.082
And that generates strong emotions.

00:01:27.082 --> 00:01:28.500
Over the last five years,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.750
we've probably had several million people

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.270
who've used virtual environments
for addressing pain,

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.448
for learning social skills

00:01:37.448 --> 00:01:41.820
if they're on the autism
spectrum to improve their mood

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.140
and to help address
social anxiety disorders.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.440
So after years of intrepid research

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.180
and positive results,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.720
can clinical virtual reality
become a mainstream component

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:53.803
of modern healthcare?

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.230
Okay, I'm just going to
make some things happen.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.270
I'm gonna set off some--

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.630
Dr. Skip Rizzo is a psychologist

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:15.900
who's been working on
clinical virtual reality

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.753
for the last 25 years.

00:02:19.373 --> 00:02:22.134
Prior to getting mixed
up with virtual reality,

00:02:22.134 --> 00:02:25.360
in the mid-nineties, I was
a clinical psychologist,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.050
and I was primarily
working in brain injury

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.760
and doing some work
with anxiety disorders.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.451
I got frustrated with
the lack of technology

00:02:34.451 --> 00:02:37.660
that could be usefully
applied in the clinical areas.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.260
And when I found out
about virtual reality,

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.310
it just made so much sense to me.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.140
We could build real world environments

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.160
that we could immerse people in,

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.000
and we could systematically
control the stimulation

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.380
or the stimuli in the environment.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.450
That to me was just an
incredible opportunity

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:02.101
for not only testing or
training or rehabilitating

00:03:02.101 --> 00:03:06.450
function in functionally
relevant environments,

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.640
but we can also maybe
introduce gaming factors

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.033
into it so that we can enhance motivation.

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:15.460
One of the main areas where VR

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.400
has been found to be clinically effective

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.850
is in helping treat
anxiety disorders and PTSD,

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.710
using VR for exposure therapy.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.110
Exposure therapy helps
a patient to confront

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:28.890
and reprocess trauma in a safe environment

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:32.280
until the symptoms connected
with the trauma are reduced.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.630
Existing strategies include imagining

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:36.973
or repeating the experience in real life.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.140
The treatment of
post-traumatic stress disorder

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:47.750
in virtual reality follows
the exact procedures

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:50.070
of the evidence-based
traditional approach,

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:54.010
except we're putting
people in VR simulations

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.410
of what they were traumatized by

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:59.563
or what they've been having
difficult experiences with.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.812
We have the visuals, we
have really good audio,

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:10.230
we also have vibration
stimuli that makes it

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.310
so you feel like you're driving a Humvee,

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.390
you're feeling the vibration.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:17.410
And we also use the sense of smell

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:20.410
where we can pop little
scents into the experience

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.600
that are reminiscent of what
that world would be like,

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.640
diesel fuel, gunpowder,

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:30.220
and all of these sensory
modalities create the experience

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:33.903
of being back in the situation
in a more realistic way.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.410
Since 2005, Dr. Rizzo has led

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.720
the Bravemind Project,

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.660
a VR therapy program that
helps veterans with combat

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.953
and sexual assault related PTSD.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:49.760
So you put the headset on them,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.500
you put them in this environment,

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.740
and you have it at a very low level

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:56.490
so that they can still feel comfortable,

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:59.770
but they know they're
getting towards confronting

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:01.860
the challenges that they've been avoiding

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.713
or traumatized by.

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:09.470
Then you ask them to recount their trauma

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:12.180
as if they're experiencing it right now.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.110
And so, speak out loud.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:18.090
I'm driving down a roadway
and heading towards Fallujah

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.390
in a Humvee and I'm
talking to my best friend,

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:25.190
and all of a sudden I see
somebody up ahead on the road

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.400
and they have a cell
phone and an IED blows up

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:29.763
the side of our vehicle.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.210
And so, they start to talk
about what they went through.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:37.040
But meanwhile, the
clinician has the capacity

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.200
to make the VR world
reflect what they're saying.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.560
So in real time, we can
make that IED go off.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.210
And so, by doing that repeatedly,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.820
and then systematically making it

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:51.930
more challenging over time,

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:55.710
you're helping them to
confront and reprocess

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:57.340
the difficult emotional memories

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:00.280
from their traumatic experience in a way

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:05.280
that actually leads to reductions
in the core PTSD symptoms.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:13.170
The science is, you know,
science is never complete.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.160
It's always growing and evolving,

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:18.170
and we're learning new things
every time we run a study,

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:21.650
but we can stand pretty
strong here in saying

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:24.630
that the use of VR to deliver
these kinds of treatment

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:29.260
does have sufficient evidence
for its clinical application.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.200
Now, Bravemind is being used

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.340
at more than a hundred military bases,

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:35.520
hospitals, and other sites.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.210
And it's proven popular.

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:39.380
People, from our research anyway,

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:41.520
are more attracted to VR

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.350
and if they see VR as part
of the therapeutic process,

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:48.960
they have more of an interest
in seeking help with VR.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.460
We've documented this with PTSD,

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:55.870
where we asked the veterans
at the start of a study,

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:58.730
if you had your choice between
the traditional therapy

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:00.860
or the VR therapy, which would you pick?

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:03.533
77% picked VR.

00:07:06.170 --> 00:07:07.940
One of the early developers of VR

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:10.570
for healthcare is Dr. Walter Greenleaf,

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:13.340
a research scientist and
behavioral neuroscientist

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:15.292
working at Stanford University's

00:07:15.292 --> 00:07:17.093
virtual human interaction lab.

00:07:18.927 --> 00:07:21.720
When I first got involved in working

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:25.500
with virtual reality
technology several decades ago,

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:28.934
the systems were exciting,

00:07:28.934 --> 00:07:31.723
but they're very different
than what we have right now.

00:07:31.723 --> 00:07:35.360
The computers that were used
were Silicon graphic machines,

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.610
and they were the size
of a small refrigerator

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.560
and cost more than half a million dollars.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.240
For a long time, virtual
reality technology

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:47.830
was viewed as scientifically
inappropriate approach,

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:49.970
that it was gaming, it was entertainment,

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:53.180
it was something from science fiction

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:54.970
and not quite here yet.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:58.830
And that's sort of caused
some people to think

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.770
it was inappropriate,
that medicine needs to be

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:02.400
much more serious

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.640
and the reality is

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:06.240
these are very powerful tools

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.083
and we could see the potential.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.950
So the digital health
revolution has been moving along

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:15.470
really for several decades,

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:17.620
but it's now just surging forward.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:21.060
And VR is one of the key components to it.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.700
It's very exciting for me
to see that we're finally

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:26.670
at a point of time where
the enabling technology

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:31.670
is both more comfortable,
very powerful and affordable.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.440
What I'm most excited about is seeing

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.420
how virtual environments
could be used to address

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:38.780
some very difficult problems

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:42.800
that we haven't had as
effective tools as we had like,

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.140
such as neuro rehabilitation

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:47.100
and cognitive rehabilitation.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.570
The ability to capture
movement dynamically,

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:53.000
to be able to build beautiful environments

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:56.860
that motivate a user to
do the difficult task

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:58.870
of recovering from a
traumatic brain injury

00:08:58.870 --> 00:08:59.980
or from a stroke.

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:01.570
Systems are also being designed

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:03.620
for friends and family to join the patient

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:05.800
during their VR experience.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:09.070
And Dr. Greenleaf hopes
these multi-user experiences

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:12.553
could be developed further to
create virtual support groups.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:19.260
Virtual environments will
allow us a very amazing way

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:23.490
to connect patients, not just
with the clinical ecosystem,

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:24.780
but with each other.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:28.710
We can have a person be in a
multi-user virtual environment,

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:31.430
but show up anonymously as an avatar.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:33.390
So that allows us in
a very comfortable way

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.070
to have support groups.

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:37.950
So for people who might be
struggling with addictions.

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:40.490
Not only can we use the
virtual environments

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:44.000
as a way to teach them
situational confidence

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.760
and learn refusal skills
and learn to get on top

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.690
of their cravings and
resist peer pressure,

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:52.610
it also allows them to
connect with other people

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:54.340
who are addressing the same challenge

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:55.660
and share their stories.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:57.810
And for someone who
might be in a small town

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.000
or who's very concerned about privacy,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.780
being able to get the sort of
support in an anonymous manner

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:06.547
that's protected is going be so important.

00:10:14.787 --> 00:10:15.620
Hey Doc.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:18.000
Yeah, I didn't know anything
about virtual reality

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.240
until about six years ago

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:23.320
when a guy named Walter
Greenleaf came to my lab,

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:26.123
and he said we have this
thing called VR, check it out.

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:29.060
Dr. Brennan Spiegel has been developing

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:31.680
VR treatments after experiencing the power

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.090
of virtual reality firsthand.

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:37.260
I realized VR has this
incredible uncanny ability

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.762
to nudge the human brain
into believing things

00:10:40.762 --> 00:10:43.940
that seem fantastical or impossible.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:47.600
And while it could be used for
entertainment or for gaming,

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.600
it also could be used
to support human health

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:53.757
in ways that we've been
discovering ever since then.

00:10:53.757 --> 00:10:56.570
Now, Dr. Spiegel and his team research

00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:58.730
and administer clinical VR tools

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:00.380
targeting pain management.

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:03.480
We've treated now over 3,000 patients

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:07.340
with VR for both acute
pain and for chronic pain.

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:09.050
So you imagine, for example,

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:11.350
somebody who's undergone an operation

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:14.610
for a bone fracture or anything,

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:18.440
and they're in the post-op
area experiencing pain.

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:22.070
And the first reaction is
to reach for medications,

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:25.950
but sometimes, you know, we
realize we have a pharmacy

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:27.010
in our own body.

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:30.050
We have natural opioids
coursing through our body.

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:33.020
And if we had a way to
deploy those opioids,

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.950
those painkillers that we
have in our spinal cord

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:39.180
without having to always
reach for another opioid,

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.380
that's what we recognize
as an opportunity.

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:43.270
You know, when we first started using VR,

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:45.620
we found that over 80% of our patients

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:47.390
found it positive or beneficial.

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:51.110
If I had a pill where 80% of
the people right off the bat

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:53.010
felt a positive experience,

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:55.950
that would be an absolute
blockbuster drug.

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:57.350
Dr. Spiegel's patient,

00:11:57.350 --> 00:11:59.700
former Air Force Colonel Tom Norris,

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:02.510
has suffered from chronic
pain for over 30 years

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:05.880
and has had positive results
through VR treatment.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:08.610
Tom puts on the VR headset
and is then immersed

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:10.830
in a virtual reality environment

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:12.780
that both distracts him from his pain

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:14.963
and teaches him useful coping tools.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.014
The first time I used VR,

00:12:18.014 --> 00:12:21.600
I was initially taken aback
because you're enclosed

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.060
in a black environment.

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:27.870
Then when they started doing
the breathing exercises,

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:29.850
which I believe was one
of the first things,

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:32.830
they had included on the headset

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:36.990
a sensor to measure your
exhalation and your inhalation.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:41.650
And the application showed
the breathing process

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:43.810
as little tiny motes of light.

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:47.518
And I got entrapped, basically,

00:12:47.518 --> 00:12:49.202
by the scene

00:12:49.202 --> 00:12:52.410
and the ability to see
my breath in motion.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.580
It sucked me right in, to be honest.

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:58.590
When you see your breath in motion,

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:01.810
and then you see an effect
caused by the motion

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:04.130
of your breath, you know, in this case,

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:07.880
it would be a tree would
bloom or colors would change,

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:11.910
it reinforces the idea that
something positive happens

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:14.183
when you breathe in a VR situation.

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:16.263
It works.

00:13:16.263 --> 00:13:17.900
I mean, there's no question about it.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.230
You can see it in the body expression.

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:21.940
You can see it in the breathing rate.

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:24.960
You can see somebody just
sort of relax into bed

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:26.810
and their blood pressure drops,

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.530
their breathing rate slows,
their heart rate slows

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:32.740
and they're having a physiologic response,

00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:34.930
not just a cognitive response,

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:37.360
but a physiologic full body reaction

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:38.963
to being in the virtual reality.

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:41.900
But while VR has been a successful tool

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:43.180
for this patient,

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:46.160
Dr. Spiegel doesn't want
people to develop dependencies

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:47.860
on virtual environments.

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:49.630
No, we don't want people to live

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:51.340
in virtual reality forever.

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:53.850
You know, we don't want people
to have to keep reaching

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:56.850
for VR the way they
might have to reach for,

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:58.480
let's say a pain pill.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:00.380
Because eventually, people will get bored

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:01.920
of being in virtual reality

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:05.240
or be so familiar with the
worlds that they're in.

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:08.690
When VR is effective, it's
actually teaching people

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:10.600
about their mind and body

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:12.800
and the connection between the two.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.450
VR is effective when we're training people

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:20.450
how to live in a real reality
or RR more effectively

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:24.250
to have a richer experience
in the real world.

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:26.580
There are also questions about how VR

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:29.705
will fit in with existing
healthcare systems.

00:14:29.705 --> 00:14:32.600
I'm sure there are some
people that are concerned

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:36.290
that the use of virtual
environments in medicine

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:39.900
will displace or replace the need for us

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:44.710
to have clinicians involved,
and that's not going to happen.

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:47.860
It's gonna be a very powerful
tool to extend their reach

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:52.092
and extend their abilities
to help their patients.

00:14:52.092 --> 00:14:54.550
If we design our systems right,

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:56.100
it should also save them time.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:57.880
I think that's something
we have to be careful with

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:00.380
as we roll out this
next wave of technology

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:04.240
there is an existing medical ecosystem

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:05.927
and anytime you bring
something new into it,

00:15:05.927 --> 00:15:09.080
you need to make sure
that it reduces costs

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:10.850
and makes things a little bit smoother

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:12.050
and a little bit easier.

00:15:14.381 --> 00:15:18.360
At this point, I don't
think we need more science.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:20.500
What we really need is insurance companies

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:24.000
to recognize the benefits
of VR and to pay for it.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.570
We need to train more clinicians

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:28.630
in how to use virtual reality.

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:31.990
We need to come up with the
protocols and procedures

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:35.850
for how to deliver this
new treatment to patients,

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:38.130
both in the hospital and as outpatients.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:39.620
And that's what our team is working on

00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:42.180
is how to actually
implement virtual reality

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:43.860
as a new branch of medicine.

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:45.930
We know that there are a lot of people

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:49.170
that have challenges in
terms of mental illness

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:52.169
that, you know, will never seek therapy.

00:15:52.169 --> 00:15:54.840
I think the World Health
Organization has estimated

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:58.300
about 600 million people
have mental health conditions

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:00.440
on the planet right now,

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:02.700
of which, fully two thirds will never see

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:04.890
the inside of a therapy office.

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:08.220
Can we use technology to get them in there

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:10.490
to get care to them in a way

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.320
where they can get over these challenges

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:14.640
that have limited what they
can do in everyday life

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:16.730
and to make their life more meaningful?

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:19.940
So that's a very exciting prospect

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:21.393
as we move into the future.

